## Room Acoustics: Why Indoor Sound Behaves Differently

Walls, ceilings and hard flooring reflect sound in a way open air never does. That is the honest inverse of our [outdoor weddings](/blog/outdoor-wedding-rental-considerations/) guide: open air disperses sound with no walls to reflect off, so an outdoor PA projects across the space, while an indoor PA is tuned to fill a room without those reflections turning speeches into an echo. A hall with hard floors and high ceilings behaves differently to a carpeted room with a lower ceiling, which is why our technician reads the actual room rather than applying one fixed setup.

## Power, Ceiling Height and Room Size

Indoor venues, hotel ballrooms, function halls and most fincas' indoor rooms, generally offer more reliable mains power than a remote outdoor site, one less point of friction than we always confirm outdoors. We still ask about power access when you book, since we do not stock generators for any venue. Ceiling height and room size matter too: a low ceiling changes how sound reflects back toward guests, and a long, narrow hall needs different speaker coverage than a square one. Our technician places stands and adjusts levels to the room you actually have, part of the live technical support included in the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/).

## Lighting as an Accent Layer Indoors

Most indoor venues already have their own lighting, chandeliers, wall sconces, house dimmers, before we arrive. The [Wedding Pack's](/packages/wedding/) fairy lights and warm LED strings sit on top of that as an accent layer, not a replacement for it. That differs from an outdoor reception after dark, where our lighting is often the only light source in the space. We do not offer venue lighting design or control of a venue's house system. Changing how the room itself is lit is a conversation for your venue directly. See our [lighting ideas for wedding rentals](/blog/lighting-ideas-for-wedding-rentals/) guide for how the fairy lights and RGBW spotlights compare between packages.

